WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.400
Since its inception in 2010, The Walking Dead has&nbsp;
earned its place as a long-running TV classic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:11.200
Despite its overwhelming popularity, though,&nbsp;
it's by no means immune to criticism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:15.920
We're here to show you some unpopular opinions&nbsp;
about The Walking Dead that raise good points.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:22.560
We're definitely not the first to notice that The&nbsp;
Walking Dead plays like an inverted Star Trek.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.680
According to the imagination of Gene Roddenberry,&nbsp;
post-current civilization humanity cooperates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.800
prospers, and revels in scientific&nbsp;
advancement and exploration. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:37.760
people in The Walking Dead universe form tribes&nbsp;
and go to war against each other, instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:43.120
pooling their resources and working together&nbsp;
against an obvious and omnipresent common threat.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.120
While Star Trek is optimistic, The&nbsp;
Walking Dead goes a step beyond pessimism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.600
into full-blown nihilism. For whoever's&nbsp;
left of the gang, their victories don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.560
really matter because they live in a world&nbsp;
that's already been conquered by walkers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.960
They just survive terrible things in&nbsp;
order to survive more terrible things.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.200
Therefore, if the series ends with anything&nbsp;
other than all the characters becoming dinner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:09.920
it will have betrayed its central themes&nbsp;
of futility and doom. Considering the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.720
that the writers have sometimes significantly&nbsp;
deviated from the graphic novels' trajectory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:18.640
there's no way of knowing if this inevitable&nbsp;
conclusion will come to pass on-screen.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:24.480
At one point in 2006's Clerks II, the&nbsp;
character of Randal Graves goes on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:30.160
little diatribe to explain his contempt for&nbsp;
the Lord of the Rings trilogy. As he puts it:

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.080
"All it was was a bunch of people walking."

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.560
Well, if you replace "walking" with&nbsp;
"killing zombies" in Randal's rant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.840
it's not an inaccurate&nbsp;
description of The Walking Dead.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.320
We're definitely not saying the show has ever&nbsp;
gotten boring. In order to get truly boring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:48.480
it would have to reduce its seasonal body&nbsp;
count to a degree that would make it a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:53.280
different show entirely. But it goes without&nbsp;
saying that things have gotten repetitive.

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:58.320
Rick and the crew struggle against the elements&nbsp;
until they find the Greene family farm. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.480
walkers overrun the farm, the heroes set out&nbsp;
on their own once again. Then they come to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:07.680
prison, and they live there until walkers take&nbsp;
over that one, too. Same deal in Alexandria. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.840
other words, The Walking Dead has a formula,&nbsp;
and it sticks to that formula pretty closely.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:17.120
If a corporation makes a successful&nbsp;
thing — it could be a TV show, film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:21.840
book, what have you — then its next step will be&nbsp;
to recreate whatever that thing happened to be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:27.280
in hopes of similar monetary rewards. In a&nbsp;
vacuum, that's not a bad impulse. It's why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:31.680
film genres exist in the first place. But&nbsp;
we've hit a point where big companies fear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:35.840
taking risks on just about anything&nbsp;
they don't think is a sure-fire hit.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.760
For proof, look no further than&nbsp;
the world of The Walking Dead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:43.520
After all, Fear the Walking Dead,&nbsp;
The Walking Dead: World Beyond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:49.840
and any other Dead-adjacent television projects in&nbsp;
development exist at the expense of an unknowable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:53.840
number of other shows that weren't greenlit&nbsp;
because they lacked a built-in audience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.560 --> 00:03:00.160
Perhaps we shouldn't judge AMC too harshly for its&nbsp;
decision-making process because that's also why we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:05.760
got Better Call Saul. But it is worth asking —&nbsp;
at what point do we no longer require expansions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:10.560
on a story that gets accused of redundancy and&nbsp;
spinning its wheels as often as The Walking Dead?

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:14.400
Are The Walking Dead fans&nbsp;
all jerks? Of course not!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.840
But there's a significant minority&nbsp;
among the fandom that's pretty toxic.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:24.560
For proof, look no further than Josh McDermitt's&nbsp;
social media experience. The actor has received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:29.360
death threats because some fans dislike the&nbsp;
character he plays — the cowardly Eugene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:35.520
Of course, if McDermitt responded in kind to each&nbsp;
and every crazed fan who threatened his life over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:39.840
the actions of an imaginary person he plays on&nbsp;
television, then McDermitt would look insane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.720
and that would interfere with his&nbsp;
ability to gain further employment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:48.640
as a Hollywood actor. His only other option was to&nbsp;
quit social media, and we can't say we blame him.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:53.520
McDermitt's far from the only cast&nbsp;
member to deal with online strife. Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:59.840
fans body-shamed Alanna Masterson, prompting both&nbsp;
the actress and costar Steven Ogg to strike back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.000
encouraging trolls to, quote, "go f---ing&nbsp;
do something productive with your life."

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:09.440
When Michonne Hawthorne first appears at the&nbsp;
end of season two, we see her leading around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:14.880
two walkers on leashes. Later, we find out that&nbsp;
these are the reanimated corpses of her boyfriend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:20.160
and her boyfriend's pal. Evidently, she mutilated&nbsp;
their bodies due to some justified resentment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.960
connected to the death of her son. But in the&nbsp;
process, Michonne discovered that walkers become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:30.800
docile and harmless once their mouths and arms are&nbsp;
removed. Even better, she learned that surrounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:35.680
herself with armless, mouthless walkers rendered&nbsp;
her invisible to actively hostile zombies.

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:42.160
So, why isn't every living person dragging a&nbsp;
pair of neutered walkers with them at all times?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.040
If there's an effective method&nbsp;
of walker deterrent available,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:48.960
why is Michonne the only person&nbsp;
who ever uses it? Furthermore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:53.360
why does she only use it when it's supposed&nbsp;
to symbolize her paranoia and isolation?

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:57.360
This is a list of opinions, of course.&nbsp;
It could very well be that people don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:01.600
want to stand next to mutilated walkers&nbsp;
24/7 because that would be a completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:04.880
disgusting way to live. But once&nbsp;
you got used to it, in our opinion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:09.280
being constantly safe from walkers is worth&nbsp;
being constantly grossed out by walkers.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:14.880
Let's talk about October of 2016. Fans&nbsp;
had waited all summer to find out whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:19.920
skull Negan would shatter at the onset of the&nbsp;
seventh season. The season premiere started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:24.720
and we all held on to our butts, anticipating a&nbsp;
payoff that would validate all our theories and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:29.280
arguments over who had the best guess.&nbsp;
After a handful of eternal minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:34.640
we watched both Abraham and Glenn face&nbsp;
immensely unpleasant blunt-force deaths.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:39.600
And it was gory. Disturbingly so. And&nbsp;
there's something strange about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:44.560
fact that the show made us wait so long,&nbsp;
made us debate and wonder and speculate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:48.960
only to treat us to a truly brutal&nbsp;
beatdown that left us all feeling queasy.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:53.360
Granted, nobody on The Walking Dead is ever&nbsp;
safe. That looming uncertainty is written into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:58.800
the genre's DNA. But in the lead-up to season&nbsp;
seven, the uncertainty gave way to a kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:04.000
sadism that one might use to promote a snuff&nbsp;
film, and that raises uncomfortable questions.

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:06.960
"You are not safe."

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:13.200
Why were we so excited to watch someone die? Is&nbsp;
there something wrong with us? Do we need help?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:16.240
And did the show step over the&nbsp;
line with those brutal killings?

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:20.720
So, if you tell us Rick Grimes seems&nbsp;
like a nice guy, you'll get no argument.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:24.240
The protagonist played by Andrew Lincoln&nbsp;
sticks by his friends and family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:26.720
and always tries to do the honorable thing.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:31.040
But, perhaps by design, he's not what&nbsp;
we'd call a fascinating individual.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:35.200
In fact, as a sheriff's deputy with&nbsp;
a wife and child in suburban Georgia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:40.320
he was living a pretty typical life before the&nbsp;
world ended. Circumstances in subsequent years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:45.040
inspired considerable intensity from Grimes,&nbsp;
but there's not a ton else that makes him feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:49.760
intrinsically unique. He didn't rise out of&nbsp;
an abusive relationship like Carol. He's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:54.560
the most charismatic person in the room at&nbsp;
all times, a la Daryl. He isn't an awesome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:59.520
swordsman like Michonne. He doesn't even have&nbsp;
a memorable gimmick like Jesus or Ezekiel.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:03.440
In some respects, he's similar to John&nbsp;
Connor in the Terminator franchise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:07.680
The other characters look to Rick Grimes as&nbsp;
a leader, and they clearly draw a tremendous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:12.880
amount of inspiration from his words and deeds.&nbsp;
But nobody ever gets all that specific about what,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:17.280
precisely, makes this guy so special.&nbsp;
Ultimately, this places Rick Grimes in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:22.160
the same category as Buffy Summers and Jerry&nbsp;
Seinfeld — thoroughly effective main characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:26.320
who are nevertheless bland as dishwater&nbsp;
when compared to all of their friends.

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:32.240
The series premiered in 2010, about a year&nbsp;
before the emergence of its contemporaries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:37.280
Game of Thrones and Once Upon a Time. These&nbsp;
ensemble dramas all felt designed to fill the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:41.360
void left by Lost -- which infamously&nbsp;
dangled solutions to the Island's many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:45.920
mysteries inches in front of its audience's&nbsp;
proverbial face throughout six seasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:50.640
Likewise, The Walking Dead gang's journey in&nbsp;
season one to the Centers for Disease Control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:55.040
and Prevention led us to assume the origin of&nbsp;
the infection might be explained at some point.

00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:00.160
Since then, many seasons have aired. And&nbsp;
in all that time, characters have rarely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:05.120
expressed interest in any big picture beyond&nbsp;
immediate necessity, and it seems unlikely that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:09.840
anybody still tuning in expects to find&nbsp;
out what started the zombie apocalypse.

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:14.000
One could argue that attaching a clear-cut&nbsp;
cause for the end of civilization undercuts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.240
the themes of random suffering and mayhem&nbsp;
that permeate throughout the rest of the show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:22.720
So if the series never explains where the walker&nbsp;
infection came from, it won't be a worse show for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:28.160
allowing its big mystery to go unsolved. In fact,&nbsp;
it's probably safer to leave that particular rock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:33.360
unturned, rather than offering up a disappointing&nbsp;
explanation that wasn't even needed to begin with.

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:40.000
As we learned in September of 2020, season eleven&nbsp;
of The Walking Dead will be its last. Regardless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:44.960
of how the spinoffs have turned out or will turn&nbsp;
out, AMC made a good decision here. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:50.080
if they made a mistake at all, it was not lopping&nbsp;
off the show's aging head a few years earlier.

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:54.480
In fact, why would AMC ever continue a&nbsp;
beloved sci-fi/horror series well after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:57.200
its quality and fan interest started to decline?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:00.960
Did AMC executives not learn the&nbsp;
tragic lesson of The X-Files?

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:06.240
When it started in 1993, The X-Files&nbsp;
became a surprise pop cultural phenomenon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:11.520
much like The Walking Dead would years later.&nbsp;
Also like The Walking Dead, The X-Files probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:16.720
should've tied off all its loose story threads&nbsp;
and called it a day sometime around season 5 or 6.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.560
Instead, the network dragged it out&nbsp;
for nine seasons and two movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:25.200
plus a revival years later. Seasons ten and&nbsp;
eleven aren't complete and total garbage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:30.240
but their worst entries — particularly the&nbsp;
cringe-inducing "My Struggle IV " — are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:35.200
bafflingly terrible to a degree that&nbsp;
damages the legacy of the entire series.

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:38.640
In its defense, The Walking Dead has&nbsp;
never produced an episode as abysmal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:43.520
as "My Struggle IV." But the show won't have&nbsp;
officially dodged that bullet until it ends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:47.760
Calling it quits at season eleven was&nbsp;
a good — albeit belated — decision.

